    short: "Severa Valmont had seized the throne from Aurelius Cassian because he refused to save their people. She performed the Separation Ritual on every citizen, watched their souls externalise into Affinities, watched the Curse recede from their flesh. They lived. For a time that was enough.",
    full: `The Fall of Severa Valmont and The Hunt for Witches

Severa Valmont had seized the throne from Aurelius Cassian because he refused to save their people. She performed the Separation Ritual on every citizen, watched their souls externalise into Affinities, watched the Curse recede from their flesh. They lived. For a time that was enough.
The first petitions came within months. Merchants argued that their Affinities gave them insights into market patterns that no ruler could match. Craftsmen claimed their elemental talents deserved recognition beyond the wages of labour. Scholars observed that if souls were external and visible, the old hierarchies of birth made no sense. Severa dismissed them, gently at first, then with the full weight of royal authority. She had not seized power to surrender it.
The idea spread. Citizens gathered in squares and taverns and marketplaces, their Affinities clustered around them like living arguments. They spoke of rule by the many rather than the one, of councils chosen by lot rather than blood, of a world where every visible soul carried equal weight. The words came from old texts, from pre-Shattering philosophies that had survived in fragments, but the feeling came from the creatures on their shoulders.
Severa ordered the gatherings dispersed. The crowds grew larger. She sent guards to arrest the speakers. The crowds turned on the guards. What began as petitions became protests became riots. Affinities fought alongside their human halves in the streets, wolf against wolf, eagle against eagle, the souls of Caelmont tearing at each other in forms that had never been meant for war.
The uprising came with fire. They stormed the palace gates, no longer with arguments but with blades. They dragged Severa from her throne and into the square where she had once stood as their saviour. They demanded she renounce her crown. She refused. They killed her where she stood, her blood spreading across the stones while her Affinity, a great bear that had walked beside her since the ritual, roared and thrashed until they killed it too.
The crowd fell silent. They had wanted rule by the many. They had not considered what rule by the many would require. In the weeks that followed they formed a council of merchants and craftsmen and scholars, men and women who had never ruled but believed they could. The council met for three months of debate before passing its first law. Within a year it had fractured into factions. Within two years the factions had armed themselves.
In the chaos that followed, a new division emerged. Those who had submitted to the Separation Ritual now looked upon those who had refused as traitors. The ones who kept their souls whole carried magic that was wilder and more powerful, unmediated by the Affinity that bound and shaped the magic of the separated. Their spells erupted with force that frightened those who had given their souls external form. They were called witches and heretics, accused of harbouring corruption that threatened the new order.
The hunts began. Armed bands moved through the districts searching for those who had never undergone the ritual. They dragged them from their homes and into the squares where the fires waited. The method of execution came from an old understanding: to kill one whose soul remained within, you must burn the soul out. Stakes rose in every quarter, and the smoke carried the smell of flesh and something else, something that made the Affinities of the watchers whine and press close to their humans for comfort.
The witches fought back. Their magic, unchecked by any external form, burned through the ranks of the hunters. The streets ran with blood from both sides. The factions that had armed themselves against each other found new purpose in the purges, each claiming to defend the true way, each burning those who disagreed. Caelmont burned in truth, not metaphor, as neighbour turned on neighbour and the smoke of a thousand stakes blotted out the sun.
The Diviners came when the fires had burned for three years. They arrived from the University of Castellenza with scrolls and ceremonies and the weight of the Mage Council behind them. They walked through streets where Affinities cowered in doorways and witches hid in cellars, and they spoke of interpretation rather than judgement. They offered a third way: those who had refused the ritual could still serve the community if they submitted to the authority of the Temples. Those who had performed the ritual could find peace in the knowledge that their souls were visible and therefore knowable.
It took decades for the fires to cool completely. In the ashes of Caelmont, where Severa Valmont's bones lay unmarked and unremembered, the Diviners established their first great Temple. They offered interpretation to those who had lost the ability to agree on what anything meant. They spoke of her fall as a warning and a lesson, though none could agree on what the lesson was. And beneath the Temple, in vaults sealed against all but the most trusted eyes, they kept the records of those years, the lists of names and the methods of burning and the desperate final words of those who had kept their souls whole and died for it.`,
